$/={mechanism_text:"Gene therapy treats disease by delivering a therapeutic gene, or by replacing, supplementing or silencing a faulty gene; it does not necessarily cut the genome at a specific DNA sequence.[2114]","CRISPR gene editing uses a guide RNA to bring a Cas nuclease to a chosen DNA site where it creates a double-strand break; repair byNHE often disrupts a gene, whileHDR can make a precise sequence change","In practice, CRISPR is a gene-editing platform that can be used inside a gene-therapy strategy, but the terms are not interchangeable",delivery_text:"Gene therapy commonly uses viral vectors especially adenoassociated virus and lentiviral systems because they efficiently carry therapeutic DNA into cells.This delivery approach can cause immunogenic toxicity and with integrating vectors insertional oncogenesis.CRISPR can be delivered by viral vectors, lipid nanoparticles, or by direct delivery of Cas protein and guide RNA as a ribonucleoprotein complex; the core challenge is getting editing machinery into the right cells long enough to act.",clinical_examples_text:"Approved gene therapy includes several established products for inherited disease and hematologic disorders such as AV-based gene replacement andiviralex vivo modification; these are now standard clinical categories rather than experimental concepts.Approved CRISPR-based therapy is now also a reality ex vivo CRISPR editing of patient cells has reached clinical approval in some regions for severe hemoglobin disorders showing that CRISPR is no longer limited to research.By contrast germline CRISPR of embryos or reproductive cells is not an approved clinical treatment and remains broadly restricted.[310]","Limitations_text:"Gene therapy may give durable benefit but it can be limited by immune responses, vector size constraints and imperfect control of where and how strongly the added gene is expressed.CRISPR offers site-specific editing but limitations include off-target edits, incomplete editing, dependence on repair pathways and difficulty achieving uniform correction in all cells.[3.Both approaches face major delivery barriers, but CRISPR adds an extra layer of risk because cut can create unintended DNA changes rather than simply adding a gene.","Ethical questions_text:"somatic treatments,germline editing,Broader also concerns also include justice,Approved_vs_experimental_text:"Approved today: conventional gene therapies and some somatic CRISPR CRISPR therapies.Experimental: highly restricted: embryo editing,Current data on long-term safety and population-level effects remain limited so many advanced CRISPR applications are still under close study rather than routine care."}
